{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-163606",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 2=6.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{64}{3}+4{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}21+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[21y+{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(63+27-21-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}\\cdot 68 \\\\  & =\\frac{34M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{34}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{3}}.\n$$",
